on></div></div></h2><p>In practice, all these 6 core concepts covered here =
are highly intertwined, and the different sections and figures cover them w=
ith different focuses or zoom levels. In the previous section, we covered p=
rompt-time use of history and how we build a compact transcript. The questi=
on there is: how much of the past should go back into the model on the next=
 turn? So the emphasis is compression, clipping, deduplication, and recency=
.</p><p>Now, this section, structured session memory, is about the storage-=
time structure of history. The question here is: what does the agent keep o=
ver time as a permanent record? So the emphasis is that the runtime keeps a=
 fuller transcript as a durable state, alongside a lighter memory layer tha=
t is smaller and gets modified and compacted rather than just appended to.<=
/p><p>To summarize, a coding agent separates state into (at least) two laye=
rs:</p><ul><li><p>working memory: the small, distilled state the agent keep=
s explicitly</p></li><li><p>a full transcript: this covers all the user req=
uests, tool outputs, and LLM responses</p></li></ul><div class=3D"captioned=
